  "Number": 36,
  "Question": "If \\(x+y=1,\\) then the largest value of \\(xy\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(0.5\\)",
    "C": "an irrational number about \\(0.4\\)",
    "D": "\\(0.25\\)",
    "E": "\\(0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The fixed sum lets us express the product using one variable: \\(y=1-x\\). Completing the square then reveals a bound:\n\\[\nxy=x(1-x)=\\frac14-\\left(x-\\frac12\\right)^2.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), the squared term is nonnegative, so \\(xy\\leq\\tfrac14\\). Equality is possible: taking \\(x=y=\\tfrac12\\) satisfies \\(x+y=1\\) and gives \\(xy=\\tfrac14\\). Thus the largest value is \\(\\boxed{0.25}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
